1. e4 { Welcome to FunnyAnimatorJimTV's Nimzowitsch Defense Study. Feel free to SHARE this study with your friends.
Please click the HEART button below if you enjoyed this study so that more people will know about it. Also you can follow to KEEP UPDATED with the studies I create. I'd really appreciate it! :)
This study is going to show you a different type of opening than the ones you see commonly in chess. The main benefit of playing openings not played very often is that your opponent won't often know how to play against the opening properly. This is a wonderful, dynamic opening played as black against 1. e4.
NOTE: I was introduced to this amazing defense by GJ_Chess on YouTube. He has several instructive videos on his channel that I highly recommend you take a look at, and he subjected this opening line to deep study and publicized his theory. His playlist of the repertoire can be found here: https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=cwjf5jexT44&list=PLz_KK5ifYNx2woG_OL0TAbtYT9qMGbBxj } *


[Event "🌵  1. e4 Nc6 - A Nice Defense 🏆: The Starting Moves"]
[Date "2017.09.02"]
[Result "*"]
[Variant "Standard"]
[ECO "B00"]
[Opening "Nimzowitsch Defense: Kennedy Variation"]
[StudyName "🌵  1. e4 Nc6 - A Nice Defense 🏆"]
[ChapterName "The Starting Moves"]
[ChapterURL "https://lichess.org/study/sEo8o4Rm/jaI4K2GP"]
[Annotator "https://lichess.org/@/FunnyAnimatorJimTV"]
[UTCDate "2017.09.02"]
[UTCTime "14:46:04"]

1. e4 { The most common opening move in chess. White takes some control of the center. Black's most common responses are shown here. But we're not going to play them! :) } { [%cal Bc7c5,Be7e5,Be7e6,Bc7c6,Bd7d5,Bd7d6] } 1... Nc6 { This is our response! 1...Nc6 is known as the Nimzowitsch Defense. The average opponent who plays e4 will be expecting something like e5, c5, d5 or e6. But we stun our opponent with this strange move! :) The average opponent will be thrown to his own resources and does not have the faintest idea how to play against it. The opponent may think something's not right because this move allows white to gain a strong pawn duo with the move d4. White can also play the move Nf3 going into the rims of King's Pawn Game, and also the move f4 going into the rims of the King's Gambit. } { [%cal Gd2d4,Yg1f3,Yf2f4] } 2. d4 { Black's knight cannot capture and white's center is ready to start steamrolling... } { [%csl Ge4,Gd4][%cal Gd1d4] } (2. f4 e5 { Now we've reached a typical position in the King's Gambit. Play continues following the K.G. theory. } { [%cal Gg1f3] }) 2... e5 { And we immediately hit back in the center with e5. This is the Kennedy Variation. The 3 main moves white can make are d5 (Linksspringer Variation, by far the most popular) dxe5 (Exchange Variation) and Nf3 (Transposing into the Scotch Game) We'll be taking a look at each of them. } { [%cal Bg1f3,Bd4e5,Rd4d5] } *


[Event "🌵  1. e4 Nc6 - A Nice Defense 🏆: Springer Variation"]
[Date "2017.09.02"]
[Result "*"]
[Variant "Standard"]
[ECO "B00"]
[Opening "Nimzowitsch Defense: Kennedy Variation, Linksspringer Variation"]
[StudyName "🌵  1. e4 Nc6 - A Nice Defense 🏆"]
[ChapterName "Springer Variation"]
[ChapterURL "https://lichess.org/study/sEo8o4Rm/2csNO2Ae"]
[Annotator "https://lichess.org/@/FunnyAnimatorJimTV"]
[UTCDate "2017.09.02"]
[UTCTime "14:50:15"]

1. e4 Nc6 2. d4 e5 { [%cal Rd4d5] } 3. d5 { This line is the most popular, logical and the most IMPORTANT to know. It's logical because white is not only gaining space on the queenside, but also gaining time on your knight. Seems good for white, no? But this is exactly what black wants! :D } (3. c3) 3... Nce7 { At this point white will probably be pretty confused, but White has a few general options. The most common is c4, although Nf3 and Nc3 are very popular and logical as well. The most aggressive is h4, but it isn't very common (There will be a chapter on it) And against all of them (except h4) we're going to be performing the same general plan. } { [%cal Yg1f3,Yb1c3,Yc2c4,Rh2h4] } 4. c4 { White is gaining even more space. } 4... Ng6 { So as we can see, what black has done is this knight has gone from b8-c6-e7-g6; from the queenside to the kingside, and the move we've just played has freed up this bishop. Even though we've used 3 moves on a single piece, this is a very important knight maneuver as we'll see later on. } { [%cal Bb8c6,Yf8b4,Bc6e7,Be7g6] } 5. Nc3 { [%cal Rf8b4] } 5... Bb4 { Black will bring his bishop out to b4, and if white allows, we will capture the knight and double white's pawns. Bringing the bishop out early also enables us to play d6 without hemming in our bishop. From here onwards the general moves/ideas we want to play will be MARKED IN BLUE. } { [%csl Rc3][%cal Rb4c3,Bc3e1,Gd7d6] } 6. Nf3 { [%cal Rb4c3] } (6. Bd2 { If white unpins the knight, you can simply wait until you're forced to exchange (a3, knight moves, etc.) Also- a GOLDEN RULE: Whenever your bishop is on b4, you NEVER PLAY d6. If you do, white can play Qa4+, winning your bishop. So you could exchange the bishop/castle and THEN play d6. } { [%csl Gd2,Gc3][%cal Gb4e1,Rd7d6,Rd1a4] } 6... Nf6 7. a3 { [%csl Rb4][%cal Ra3b4] } 7... Bxc3 { Since white forced our bishop to move with a3 we captured then. The reason why we wait is so that white will have to waste a move to get rid of our bishop- so we're a move ahead. :) } 8. Bxc3 { Here white has avoided getting doubled pawns, however our plan is going to be extremely similar to the mainline plan (further on in the chapter) }) 6... Bxc3+ { [%csl Bc3][%cal Bb4c3] } 7. bxc3 { [%csl Rc4,Rc3] } 7... d6 { Now we play d6. We create a pawn chain aiming at the kingside and we control the c5 square. } { [%csl Bd6,Gc8][%cal Bd6c5] } 8. Bd3 { [%cal Ge1g1] } 8... Nf6 { [%csl Bf6][%cal Ye8g8] } 9. O-O O-O { [%csl Bg8][%cal Be8g8] } 10. Bg5 { [%csl Yf6][%cal Yg5d8] } 10... h6 { If white ever brings his bishop down and pins the knight, you should immediately kick it back. } { [%csl Bg5][%cal Bh6g5] } 11. Be3 { White is threatening to play c5 and open up some lanes for himself on the queenside!! GOLDEN RULE: You should never let white expand on the queenside with c5. To do this... } { [%csl Rc5][%cal Rc4c5,Ye3c5] } 11... b6 { Now the c5 square has been completely blocked. } { [%csl Bb6,Bc5][%cal Bb6c5] } 12. a4 { White insists on opening up the queenside with an a5 pawn break. } { [%csl Rb6][%cal Ra4a5,Ra5b6] } 12... a5 { We play a5, blocking his pawn. And as we can see we have created this *lovely* pawn structure, and it's COMPLETELY cramping white's queenside. This leaves us to CONCENTRATE on our kingside attack without worrying about white on the queenside. } { [%csl Rd5,Rc4,Rc3,Ra4,Ba5,Bb6,Bc7,Bd6,Be5] } 13. Rb1 { The goal from here is... Black wants to move his f6 knight either to h5 or h7 (moving to h7 will have to follow an h6 pawn push if you haven't done that already), and make way for his f-pawn to thrust to f5. You can play your knight to h5 ONLY IF white has a loose bishop on d3. If white's bishop is protected well, then you can't play Nh5. In this case you'll have to play Nh7, which is a slower plan, but it works. To see the Nh7 plan in action, click the sideline on the moves list on the right -->> } { [%csl Yf7,Rd3,Gh7][%cal Bf6h7,Bf6h5,Yf7f5,Gh7h6] } 13... Nh5 { Nh5 is a safe move IN THIS CASE. Now, whenever you play Nh5, you have to be aware that your E5 PAWN is now under attack. However, the pawn is safe here, and the reason is- white has a LOOSE BISHOP on D3. To see what happens if white takes the pawn here, check out the sideline on the moves list. -->> } { [%csl Bh5,Rd3,Ge5][%cal Gf3e5,Yd1h5] } (13... Nh7 { [%cal Gf7f5] } 14. Re1 f5 { You know the plan :) } { [%csl Bf5][%cal Bf7f5] } 15. exf5 Bxf5 16. Bxf5 Rxf5 { One pair of bishops are exchanged. } 17. Qd3 { [%csl Rf5,Rg6][%cal Rd3g6] } 17... Rf6 { Here you proceed with protecting your pieces, bringing your queen to the kingside via either e8 or d7, and bringing the other rook to f8. You can activate your knights however they can coordinate easily, and the f4 outpost is still available to you. } { [%csl Ba8,Gf4][%cal Ba8f8,Yd8d7,Yd7h3,Gg6f4,Gf6f4] }) 14. Qd2 { The goal of our WHOLE SETUP is to place one of the knights on the wonderful f4 square. } { [%csl Bf4][%cal Bh5f4,Bg6f4] } (14. Nxe5 { This looks like white is winning a pawn... But wait a second! } { [%csl Rh5,Ge5][%cal Rd1h5,Gf3e5] } 14... Nxe5 { [%cal Gd1h5,Be5d3] } 15. Qxh5 Nxd3 { It turns out it's white who has lost a piece. THE MORAL IS you can SAFELY play your knight to h5 if white has a loose bishop on d3. } { [%csl Gc8,Gd3,Re3] } (15... Bg4 { [%csl Rh5][%cal Rg4h5] })) 14... Nhf4 { Now we slot our knight into the WONDERFUL square f4. If you can manage your knight to place on f4, essentially 50% of your game has been won. :) } { [%csl Bf4][%cal Bh5f4] } 15. Bxf4 Nxf4 { Now we have our other knight to support f4 as well. At any time of all of this we can develop our bishop to either d7 or g4. } { [%cal Gc8d7,Gc8g4] } 16. Bc2 { [%cal Yf7f5] } 16... f5 17. exf5 Bxf5 18. Bxf5 Rxf5 19. Qc2 { [%cal Rc2f5] } 19... Qf6 { And we have very simple ideas from here. Put the queen on an aggressive square and join the final rook in the party. :) This is a quite slow but very effective plan for the kingside attack. Most opponents are helpless opposing this plan. KEEP IN MIND that throughout the course of the middlegame you may not be able to perform certain moves because of how your opponent has set up. In these cases you'll have to use some intuition to move on. Also watch out for tactics during the game. :) } { [%cal Gf6g6,Ga8f8] } *


[Event "🌵  1. e4 Nc6 - A Nice Defense 🏆: Colorado Gambit Accepted"]
[Date "2017.09.02"]
[Result "*"]
[Variant "Standard"]
[ECO "B00"]
[Opening "Nimzowitsch Defense: Colorado Countergambit Accepted"]
[StudyName "🌵  1. e4 Nc6 - A Nice Defense 🏆"]
[ChapterName "Colorado Gambit Accepted"]
[ChapterURL "https://lichess.org/study/sEo8o4Rm/izmYXB81"]
[Annotator "https://lichess.org/@/FunnyAnimatorJimTV"]
[UTCDate "2017.09.02"]
[UTCTime "14:49:36"]

{ The Colorado Gambit is a really fun opening that will probably confuse the heck out of your opponent. In most lines it allows black to have a good control of the center, can seize the initiative from the white early on, black has a flexible piece play and in some lines black gets a vigorous attack against white. }
1. e4 Nc6 2. Nf3 { Nf3 has a pretty clever idea, in that black has the obvious option to play the move e5, in which white will be back into his familiar territory (Italian, Spanish, Scotch, etc.)... But here comes the big surprise. And the surprise is... } { [%cal Ge7e5] } 2... f5 { f5! And white will probably be bewildered. At first sight it looks ridiculous; we're offering a pawn, and we're opening up a diagonal to our king. But we are able to neutralize both of these later on. White has several options at this point. The two main options are the Accepted (exf5) and Declined (e5). Check the sidelines at this move to see the recommended procedures against Bc4 and Nc3. } { [%csl Rf5][%cal Ye4e5,Yf1c4,Yb1c3,Ge4f5] } (2... e5 3. Bc4 Bc5 4. O-O Nf6 5. d4 exd4 6. e5) 3. exf5 { [%csl Rf5][%cal Re4f5] } (3. Bc4 { This move is an inaccuracy but quite a few people play it. } 3... fxe4 { We immediately win material and gain time and space. } { [%csl Rf3][%cal Re4f3] } 4. Bxg8 { [%csl Rf3][%cal Re4f3,Gc4g8] } (4. Ng5 { [%csl Yf7][%cal Yg5f7,Yc4f7] } 4... d5 { [%csl Rc4][%cal Rd5c4] } 5. Bb5 Nf6 { [%cal Be7e5,Bc8d7,Bf8e7,Ye8g8,Rh7h6,Ra7a6] }) 4... exf3 5. Bd5 { [%cal Rd5g2] } 5... fxg2 6. Bxg2 g6 { Simple ideas for black; white has crippled kingside pawns. } { [%csl Rh2,Rf2][%cal Gf8g7,Ge7e5,Gd7d5,Be8g8] }) (3. Nc3 { This is one of the lines where black can gain a nice center. } { [%csl Re4] } 3... fxe4 4. Nxe4 d5 { Black gains time and space. } { [%csl Re4][%cal Rd5e4] } 5. Ng3 { If Nc3, black's ...d4 gains even more time and space. } 5... e5 { White has let black acquire a pawn duo in the center. Black has open development and white has had to waste time with his knights. } { [%csl Ge5,Gd5] } 6. Bb5 Bg4 7. h3 Bxf3 8. Qxf3 Nf6 9. Nh5 { [%csl Rf6][%cal Rh5f6] } 9... Qd6 { [%cal Gd6f6,Gd6c6] } 10. Nxf6+ gxf6 11. Qh5+ { [%csl Re8][%cal Rh5e8] } 11... Kd7 { Looks like we are disturbing the castling, but see that our rook can come to d8 and the king will tuck in the queenside and works as an artificial castle. And we have the huge pawn center. } { [%csl Rf6,Re5,Rd5][%cal Yd7c8,Ga8d8] }) 3... d5 { Black is immediately trying to regain the pawn. } { [%csl Bf5][%cal Bc8f5] } 4. d4 { This is the main line. } (4. Bd3 { 1st way white can try to hold on to the pawn. } { [%csl Gf5][%cal Gd3f5] } 4... Nh6 { [%csl Rf5][%cal Rh6f5,Rc8f5] } 5. Nh4 { [%csl Gf5][%cal Gh4f5,Gd3f5] } 5... e5 { [%cal Rf5e6,Gd8h4] } 6. Qh5+ { [%cal Rh5e8] } 6... Nf7 { [%csl Gf7] } 7. Nc3 g6 8. Qg4 { [%csl Gh4][%cal Gg4h4] } (8. fxg6 hxg6 { [%csl Gh4][%cal Gh8h4,Gd8h4] }) 8... g5 { [%csl Rh4] } 9. Nf3 e4 { [%csl Rf3,Rd3][%cal Re4f3,Re4d3] } 10. Bxe4 h5 { [%csl Rg4] } 11. Qg3 Bd6 { [%cal Rd6g3] } 12. Qh3 dxe4 { Disgusting position for white. White is threatening trapping white's queen with g4 so white has no time to capture on e4 - he'll lose the f3 knight. } { [%cal Rg5g4] }) (4. Nh4 { 2nd attempt at holding the pawn. } { [%csl Gf5][%cal Gh4f5,Yd1h5] } 4... Nh6 { [%csl Rf5][%cal Rh6f5] } 5. Qh5+ g6 { This move looks suicidal, but it turns out very well. } 6. fxg6 hxg6 7. Qxg6+ (7. Nxg6 Bg4 { [%csl Rh5] } 8. Qg5 (8. Qh4 Qd6 { [%csl Rg6][%cal Rd6e5,Rd6g6] } 9. Nxh8 Qe5+ 10. Be2 Qxe2#) 8... Rg8 { [%csl Rg6][%cal Rg8g5,Yd8d6,Ge8c8] }) 7... Nf7 { [%csl Rh4] } 8. Nf3 Rh6 { Now we start attacking the queen. } { [%csl Rg6] } 9. Qg3 Bf5 { [%csl Gg6][%cal Gf5g6] } 10. d4 Rg6 { [%csl Rg3] } 11. Qh4 Qd7 { [%csl Bg2,Bf1,Gc2][%cal Rg6g2,Rf5c2,Gc6b4] } 12. Be3 { [%cal Ge8c8] } 12... O-O-O 13. Nc3 e6 14. Nb5 Be7 { [%csl Rh4][%cal Re7h4] } 15. Qf4 Rg4 { [%csl Rf4][%cal Yg8g2,Yh8h2,Gd8h8] }) 4... Bxf5 { [%cal Rf1b5] } 5. Bb5 { This line is the most aggressive one that white can play against the Colorado. } 5... e6 { [%cal Yg8f6,Yf8d6] } 6. Ne5 { [%csl Rc6][%cal Re5c6,Rb5e8] } 6... Ne7 { [%csl Gc6][%cal Ge7c6] } 7. Qh5+ { [%cal Rh5e8] } 7... g6 8. Qe2 Bg7 { [%csl Be5][%cal Bg7e5] } 9. Bg5 { [%csl Re7,Rc6][%cal Bg5d8,Bb5e8] } (9. g4 { This move looks like it's trapping the bishop, but black can gain material here. } 9... Be4 10. f3 { [%csl Re4] } 10... O-O 11. fxe4 Nxd4 { [%csl Ge2,Gb5,Ge5][%cal Gd4e2,Gd4b5,Gg7e5] }) 9... Qd6 { Supporting the c6 knight and also unpinning the e7 knight. } { [%cal Gd8d6] } 10. Bxe7 Kxe7 11. Bxc6 bxc6 12. f4 Rab8 { [%cal Bb8b2] } 13. c3 { Black's h8 rook can swing anywhere, and black's king will walk to the g8. Black also has the bishop pair. This concludes the Colorado Gambit Accepted and most of the lines and traps. } { [%csl Yg7,Yf5][%cal Gh8c8,Re7f8,Rf8g8] } *


