{ The declined variation is the 2nd most popular response to the Colorado. It can allow black to gain attacking positions as well as central control. } { [%cal Ge4e5,Ye4f5,Yb1c3,Yf1c4] }
1. e5 { Pushing by. } { [%cal Re4e5] } 1... d6 { Challenging the pawn. } { [%csl Be5][%cal Bd6e5] } 2. exd6 (2. Bb5 { This is one of two general moves by white. It's quite aggressive but the line contains a trap. } { [%csl Rc6][%cal Rb5e8] } 2... dxe5 3. Nxe5 { White will be more than happy to get this position. } { [%csl Rc6][%cal Re5c6,Rb5e8] } 3... Qd5 { And white has fallen into the trap :) } { [%csl Yb5,Ye5][%cal Yd5e5,Yd5b5] } 4. Bxc6+ bxc6 { [%csl Re5] } 5. Nf3 { This is the best move. } (5. Qh5+ { Several people might play this, } 5... g6 6. Nxg6 { This looks winning for white, but black has something up his sleeve. } { [%csl Bh8][%cal Bg6h8,Rh5h8,Rh7g6] } 6... Nf6 { [%csl Rh5][%cal Rf6h5] } 7. Qh4 { If the queen moves from the h-file, the knight drops. } { [%csl Rh7][%cal Rh4h8] } 7... Qxg2 { [%csl Rh1,Rg6][%cal Rg2h1,Rg2g6] } 8. Nxh8 { White attempts to keep material even. } 8... Qxh1+ 9. Ke2 { Instead of grabbing that bishop in the corner right away, we have a very nice move which is... } { [%csl Yc1][%cal Yh1c1] } 9... f4 10. Nc3 { Now white has gotten the chance to defend the bishop, but we have a checkmate attack now. } { [%csl Gc1][%cal Ga1c1] } 10... Bg4+ 11. Kd3 Qf1+ 12. Kd4 Rd8+ 13. Kc5 e5+ { [%cal Gf8c5] } 14. Kxc6 Qa6+ 15. Kxc7 Qb6# { [%cal Re1e2,Re2d3,Rd3d4,Rd4c5,Rc5c6,Rc6c7] }) 5... Qe6+ { [%cal Re6e1] } 6. Qe2 { [%csl Be2] } 6... Qxe2+ 7. Kxe2 { [%csl Re2][%cal Gc8a6] } 7... e6 { Black has easy development and white's king is in an awkward position. } { [%csl Re2][%cal Gc8a6,Gf8c5,Gg8f6,Ye8g8,Bb8b3,Bd8d3] }) 2... cxd6 { Now we have two central pawns to white's one. White has the passive Be2 and the more aggressive Nc3. } { [%cal Yf1e2,Yb1c3] } 3. Nc3 (3. Be2 { [%cal Ge1g1] } 3... Nf6 4. O-O e5 { Now we push e5 and we have the center. Our threat is e5 so white needs to stop that. } { [%cal Re5e4] } 5. d3 d5 { We push the pawn and we have complete control of the center. How often do you get a position like this? Black has more space and the plan is very simple- black is playing Bd6, castling and starting a kingside attack with f5. Black clearly has the advantage. } { [%csl Yd5,Ye5,Yf5][%cal Bf8d6,Be8g8,Rf5f4] }) 3... e5 { We gain the center. } 4. d4 { The main move. } (4. Bc4 { This is an inaccuracy. True it gains the complete diagonal, but... } { [%cal Gc4g8] } 4... e4 { And that knight doesn't have any good squares, so... it has to go back. XD Our followup will be Nf6 and d5. } { [%csl Bh4,Bg5,Be5,Bd4][%cal Rf3g1,Gg8f6,Gd6d5] }) 4... Nf6 5. d5 Nb4 6. Bb5+ Bd7 7. Bxd7+ Qxd7 8. a3 Na6 { This is actually a pretty good spot for knight. It may look unappealing, but the knight can jump to either c7 or c5 in accordance with what white does. For example... } { [%csl Ga6][%cal Ga6c7,Ga6c5] } 9. Ng5 { As we can see white is looking at the juicy e6 square... } { [%csl Ye6][%cal Yg5e6] } 9... Nc7 { But we can nicely cover that square with our knight. } { [%csl Be6][%cal Bc7e6] } 10. O-O Be7 11. Qd3 O-O { [%csl Ye5,Yf5][%cal Rh7h6] } *


