{ At this point the MOST AGGRESSIVE response by white is the h4 push. } { [%cal Rh2h4,Yg1f3,Yb1c3,Yc2c4] }
1. h4 { By playing h4, if black puts his knight on g6, white can immediately kick that knight. We can deal with this by thrusting in the center. } { [%cal Ge7g6,Rh4h5,Rh5g6] } 1... f5 { [%csl Re4][%cal Yf7f5,Rf5e4] } 2. Nc3 Nf6 3. Bg5 { [%csl Re7,Rf6][%cal Rg5d8] } 3... Nxe4 4. Nxe4 fxe4 5. Qh5+ { White wants to weaken black's kingside. } { [%csl Re8][%cal Rh5e8,Yg7g6] } 5... g6 6. Qe2 { [%csl Be4][%cal Be2e4] } 6... d6 { [%csl Gc7,Gd6,Ge5] } 7. h5 { This looks like a very threatening move. } { [%csl Rh5] } 7... Qd7 { The best response. } 8. Bxe7 Bxe7 9. hxg6 Qg4 { [%csl Ge2,Gg6][%cal Gg4e2,Gg4g6] } 10. Qxg4 Bxg4 11. gxh7 { White has a pawn on the 7th rank, but this pawn is blockaded and can easily be caught. } 11... Kf7 { It's safe enough to activate the king. } { [%cal Ge8f7,Gf7g7] } 12. Be2 { [%csl Rg4][%cal Re2g4] } 12... Bf5 { [%csl Gh7][%cal Gf5h7] } 13. Bh5+ Kg7 { [%csl Bh7][%cal Bg7h7,Bh8h7,Bf5h7] } *


