1. e4 Nc6 2. d4 { [%csl Ge4,Gd4] } 2... e5 { [%cal Re5d4] } 3. Nf3 { And now we've transposed into a Scotch game, as if white had just played d4. } { [%cal Gd2d4] } 3... exd4 { White can play the Scotch Gambit with Bc4 and black can try to hold on to his pawn, but the mainline is where white recaptures. To see the tree of moves for the Scotch Gambit, click the massive sideline on the moves list on the right. } { [%cal Rf1c4,Gf3d4] } 4. Nxd4 (4. Bc4 Bc5 { [%cal Rc2c3,Gc1g5,Ge1g1] } 5. c3 { [%cal Rd4c3] } 5... Nf6 { [%cal Gc3d4,Ge1g1,Gc1g5,Re4e5] } (5... dxc3 6. Bxf7+ Kxf7 7. Qd5+ { [%csl Rc5,Rf7][%cal Gd5f7,Gd5c5] }) 6. e5 { The mainline. } (6. Bg5 h6 { [%csl Rg5] } 7. Bh4 { If Bxf6 Qxf6 black is completing his development with a solid support for his d4 pawn. } 7... g5 8. Bg3 Nxe4) (6. O-O Nxe4 { Don't be afraid of the pin. } 7. Re1 { [%csl Re4] } 7... d5 8. cxd4 Bb4 { [%cal Rb4e1] } 9. Bd2 O-O { [%csl Yd5][%cal Gc8e6,Gc8f5,Gc8g4] }) (6. cxd4 Bb4+ { [%cal Gc1d2,Gb1c3] } 7. Bd2 { [%csl Ye4][%cal Yf6e4] } (7. Nc3 { [%csl Ye4] } 7... Nxe4 { [%csl Rc3] } 8. O-O Bxc3 9. d5 { [%csl Rc3,Rc6] } 9... Bf6 10. dxc6 (10. Re1 { [%csl Be4,Bc6][%cal Be1e4,Bd5c6] } 10... Ne7 11. Rxe4 d6 { Black has a better pawn structure and is a pawn up. } { [%csl Gc8,Rd5] }) 10... bxc6 11. Re1 O-O { [%csl Re4] } 12. Rxe4 d5 { [%csl Be4,Bc4][%cal Bd5e4,Bd5c4] }) 7... Nxe4 8. Bxb4 Nxb4 9. Bxf7+ Kxf7 10. Qb3+ { [%cal Yb3f7,Yb3b4] } 10... d5 11. Qxb4 Re8 { [%cal Re8e1] } 12. O-O c6 { [%csl Gb7,Gc6,Gd5][%cal Gd8a5] } 13. Ne5+ Kg8 14. Nc3 Qb6 15. Qxb6 axb6 16. Nxe4 dxe4 { Black's pawns are crippled, but they can be easily used and defended. } { [%csl Re4,Rb6,Rb7,Rc6] } 17. Rfe1 Be6 { [%csl Ge4,Ya2][%cal Ge1e4,Ye6a2,Ya8a2] } 18. a3 Bd5 { [%csl Gc6,Gd5,Ge4,Gb7,Re5,Rd4] }) 6... d5 { [%cal Ge5f6,Rc4b5] } 7. Bb5 (7. exf6 dxc4 8. fxg7 Rg8 { [%csl Yd4,Yc4,Yc5,Yc6][%cal Gc1h6,Rc1g5,Ge1g1] } 9. Bg5 (9. Bh6 Qf6 { [%csl Rh6,Gd4][%cal Rf6h6] } 10. Qe2+ Be6 11. Bg5 Qxg7 { Black has a fantastic position here. A wonderful center, open g-file and black can castle long. } { [%csl Yc4,Yd4][%cal Ge8c8,Gd4d3] }) (9. O-O Rxg7 10. Re1+ Be6 11. Bf4 Qd7 12. Bg5 { [%csl Rd8][%cal Gg5d8] } 12... d3 { [%csl Yd3] } 13. Nbd2 Be7 14. Bxe7 Qxe7 15. Nxc4 O-O-O) 9... f6 { [%csl Rg5][%cal Rf6g5] } 10. Qe2+ Qe7 11. Bxf6 Qxe2+ 12. Kxe2 Bg4 { [%csl Rf3] } 13. Nd2 d3+ 14. Kf1 Bxf3 15. Nxf3 Kf7 { [%csl Rf6] } 16. Bh4 Kxg7 { The pawn majorities are complex, but black has this wonderful passed pawn on d3. } { [%csl Yd3,Yc4] }) 7... Ne4 8. cxd4 Bb6 { [%csl Rg4][%cal Gc8g4] } 9. Be3 O-O 10. O-O { [%cal Gf7f5] } 10... f5 11. exf6 Nxf6 12. Qc2 Qd6 { White has two general knight moves. From here we place our bishop on d7 and we can get either the bishop pair or huge attack. } { [%csl Ga8][%cal Gb1d2,Gb1c3,Yc8d7] }) 4... Bc5 5. Be3 Qf6 6. c3 (6. Nb5 Bxe3 7. Nxc7+ (7. fxe3 Qh4+ 8. g3 Qd8 9. Qg4 g6 10. Qf4 d6 11. N1c3 a6 12. Nd4 Ne5) 7... Kd8 8. Nxa8) 6... Nge7 7. Bc4 O-O 8. O-O d6 { [%cal Gc8d7,Gc8e6,Gc8g4,Yf8e8,Ya8d8] } *


