1. e4 Nc6 2. d4 e5 { The Exchange Variation is not very common, but every once in a while you'll see it. } { [%cal Gd4d5,Rd4e5,Gg1f3] } 3. dxe5 Nxe5 { These are white's main options. } { [%cal Gg1f3,Yf2f4,Yb1c3,Yc1f4] } 4. Nf3 Qf6 { We are now threatening to double white's pawns. There are two ways he can deal with this. } { [%cal Rf3e5,Rf1e2] } 5. Nxe5 (5. Be2 { [%csl Gf3] } 5... Bb4+ { If Bd2, after ...Nxf3+, Bxf3 we have Qxb2 winning a pawn with a better position. } { [%cal Rc1d2,Gb1d2,Gc2c3] } 6. Nbd2 { From here on, the plan is very similar to the Springer Variation plan. You will play Ne7-g6 and target the juicy f4 square, castle short, d6, and the other bishop can go to either d7 or g4. } { [%csl Gf4][%cal Ye8g8,Bg8e7,Be7g6,Yc8d7,Yc8g4,Bd7d6] } (6. c3 Bc5 { [%csl Rc3,Gf4][%cal Be7g6,Ye8g8,Yc8d7,Yc8g4,Bd7d6,Bg8e7,Rb1c3] }) 6... Ne7 7. c3 { [%csl Rb4][%cal Rc3b4] } 7... Bc5 { If your opponent plays the "aggressive move" b4, you can simply capture that pawn and his c-pawn cannot capture the bishop after Nxf3+, winning the rook on a1. } { [%csl Ya1][%cal Rb2b4] } 8. O-O (8. b4 { [%csl Bc5] } 8... Bxb4 { [%csl Ge1][%cal Rc3b4,Ge5f3,Yf6a1] } 9. Qb3 Bxc3 { Again the queen cannot capture the bishop because of Nxf3+. } { [%csl Ra1][%cal Rb3c3,Ge5f3,Yf6c3,Rc3a1] } 10. Rb1 Nxf3+ 11. Bxf3 { Black is two pawns up. } { [%csl Yc7,Yb7] }) 8... d6 { Continuing our main moves. } { [%csl Yc8] } 9. Nb3 { [%csl Bc5][%cal Bb3c5] } 9... Bb6 10. Kh1 { Kh1 unpins white's f-pawn and his idea is to capture our knight and then play f4. You have to get used to this kind of scenario. We can defuse this by playing... } { [%cal Rb6g1,Yf2f4,Gf3e5] } 10... Nxf3 11. Bxf3 O-O { Simple. } 12. Be2 { [%cal Yf2f4] } 12... Qe5 { Golden rule: Whenever white wants to push f4, pressurize the e4 pawn. } { [%csl Be4][%cal Be5e4] } 13. Qc2 f5 14. Bd3 { [%csl Ge4] } 14... fxe4 15. Bxe4 Bf5 { We just want to exchange the pieces because our position is very good for the endgame. } { [%csl Ye4][%cal Yf5e4] } 16. Bxf5 Qxf5 17. Qxf5 Nxf5 { [%cal Ya8e8] } 18. f3 { White wants to free up the rook, but this is not a good move; it allows us to dominate with our rooks and bishop. } { [%csl Gf1][%cal Rb6g1] } 18... Rae8 19. Bg5 { Another error. } 19... Re2 { Now our rooks is incredibly active on the 7TH RANK. } { [%csl Gb2][%cal Ge2g2,Ge2b2] } 20. Rab1 h6 { [%csl Rh4] } 21. Bd2 Nh4 { [%csl Bg2,Rg1][%cal Bh4g2,Be2g2,Rb6g1] } 22. g3 Nxf3 { Black will either checkmate white on h2 or win material. Black is winning tremendously. } { [%csl Bd2,Rh2][%cal Bf3d2,Rf3h2,Re2h2,Be2d2] }) 5... Qxe5 { We are threatening his pawn. White has two good options. } { [%csl Re4][%cal Re5e4,Gf1d3,Gb1c3] } 6. Bd3 (6. Nc3 Bb4 { [%csl Rc3][%cal Yf1d3,Yc1d2] } 7. Bd2 Nf6 8. Bd3 { Here, if you want to play a very aggressive game you can go for d5, but it can be very risky because your queen and king are in line and it could be very disaster-like. } { [%csl Ye5,Ye8][%cal Ge1g1,Rd7d5] } 8... d6 9. O-O Bg4 10. f3 { [%csl Gg4][%cal Yf3g4] } 10... Bc5+ { [%cal Rc5g1] } 11. Kh1 Bd7 12. f4 { [%csl Ye5][%cal Yf4e5] } 12... Qe7 { In the Exchange Variation, whenever WHITE PLAYS F4 attacking your queen, the BEST SQUARE for her is E7. } 13. Qf3 { [%csl Gf4,Ge4,Yf1,Ya1][%cal Rd2h6,Rd3h7,Re8g8,Ge8c8] } 13... O-O-O { [%csl Gc8] } 14. Be3 Bxe3 15. Qxe3 Kb8 16. a4 { [%cal Ra2a4,Rb2b4,Ra4a5] } 16... d5 { White's e4 pawn is pinned. } { [%csl Re4] } 17. e5 Ng4 { [%cal Bg4e3] } 18. Qf3 d4 { [%cal Yc3d5,Ye7h4] } 19. Ne4 f5 { [%cal Re5f6,Gg7f6,Yg8g2] } 20. Ng5 h6 21. Nh3 { In the last few moves, all white was doing was moving his knight around and it ends up on the rim. xD Meanwhile, the juicy e3 square has been weakened, and we exploit this. } { [%csl Re3][%cal Bc3e4,Be4g5,Bg5h3] } 21... Ne3 { [%csl Rf1][%cal Re3f1] } 22. Rfe1 Bc6 { not only attacking the queen, but we are threatening g2 as well. Black has a fantastic position here. } { [%csl Rf3,Gg2][%cal Yc6g2,Ye3g2] }) 6... Bb4+ { White cannot play Bd2 because of Qxb2. } { [%csl Rb2][%cal Rc1d2,Re5b2,Gb1d2,Gc2c3] } 7. c3 (7. Nd2 { [%cal Bg8f6,Ye8g8,Bd7d6,Yc8d7] } 7... Nf6 8. O-O d6 9. f4 { Remember that whenever white plays f4, place the queen on E7. } { [%cal Yf2f4] } 9... Qe7 { [%csl Ge7] } 10. c3 { [%csl Rb4][%cal Rc3b4] } (10. Nf3 { White offers a pawn for the notion that black will have danger with the e-file pin. } { [%csl Re4][%cal Ye1e8] } 10... Nxe4 11. Bxe4 Qxe4 { [%csl Re4,Re8,Ge1][%cal Gb4e1] } 12. c3 { [%csl Be8,Be4,Gb4][%cal Rf1e1,Re1e8,Gc3b4] } 12... Bc5+ { [%csl Gg1][%cal Gc5g1] } 13. Kh1 O-O { [%csl Gd6] }) 10... Bc5+ 11. Kh1 O-O 12. Qe1 { [%cal Ge1h4,Ge1g3] } 12... Re8 { [%csl Ye4][%cal Ye8e4] } 13. b4 { [%csl Rc5] } 13... Bb6 14. Bb2 a5 { [%csl Gb4][%cal Ga5b4] } 15. a3 Bd7 { [%csl Bd7,Be8,Be7,Bf6,Bb6] } 16. c4 { [%csl Yf6][%cal Yb2f6] } 16... axb4 17. axb4 Rxa1 18. Bxa1 Ng4 { [%csl Ge3][%cal Gb6e3,Gg4e3] } 19. Rf3 { [%csl Be3][%cal Bf3e3] } 19... f5 { [%cal Ge4f5,Ye8e1] } 20. h3 fxe4 { [%csl Bf3,Bd3][%cal Be4f3,Be4d3,Gh3g4] } 21. Nxe4 Bc6 { [%csl Re4,Gg4][%cal Rc6e4,Re8e4] } 22. hxg4 Bxe4 23. Bxe4 Qxe4 24. Qc3 { [%csl Rg7][%cal Ra1g7] } 24... Qe1+ 25. Kh2 Bg1+ 26. Kh3 Qxc3 27. Rxc3 Bd4 { [%csl Ya1,Yc3][%cal Yd4a1] } 28. Rc1 Bxa1 29. Rxa1 Re4 { [%csl Bf4,Bc4][%cal Be4f4,Be4c4] } 30. Ra7 { [%csl Rb7][%cal Ra7e7] } 30... Rxf4 31. Rxb7 Rxc4 { [%csl Gd6][%cal Gd6d1] }) 7... Bc5 8. O-O Nf6 { [%cal Bf6e4] } 9. Kh1 { White has unpinned his f-pawn, so he is most likely going to play f4 next move. } { [%cal Yf2f4] } 9... d6 10. f4 { [%csl Be5][%cal Bf4e5] } 10... Qe7 { Golden Rule: In the Exchange Variation, whenever WHITE PLAYS F4 attacking your queen, the BEST SQUARE for her is E7. } { [%csl Ge7] } 11. Nd2 O-O { It's dangerous to play for queenside castle because white has committed c3, and when we castle queenside the b4 and a4 can come in a flash and white will have a queenside pawn storm. So don't castle queenside if white has played c3. } { [%csl Rc3][%cal Rb2b4,Ra2a4] } 12. Qf3 Bd7 13. b4 { [%csl Bc5][%cal Bb4c5] } 13... Bb6 14. a4 { Threatening h5, trapping our bishop. } { [%cal Ra4a5] } 14... a5 { [%cal Ga7a5,Ra5b4] } 15. b5 { [%csl Rb5,Ra4,Ga5] } 15... Ng4 { Eyeing the wonderful e3 square for the knight. } { [%csl Be3][%cal Bg4e3,Bb6e3] } 16. Nc4 { Seems like white is creating a counter-threat, but this is a blunder, because of... } { [%csl Rb6][%cal Rc4b6] } 16... Nxh2 { BAM! The king cannot capture because of Qh4+ } { [%csl Bf3,Bf1][%cal Bh2f3,Bh2f1] } 17. Qh5 (17. Kxh2 Qh4+ { [%cal Gb6g1,Gh4h1,Rf3h3] } 18. Qh3 Bxh3) 17... Nxf1 18. e5 { [%csl Rh7][%cal Rh5h7,Rd3h7] } 18... Ng3+ { [%csl Bh1,Bh5][%cal Bg3h1,Bg3h5] } *


