1. e4 Nc6 2. Bc4 { This move is an inaccuracy, but it's still played quite often. } 2... Nf6 { There will be a transposition, so the move white chooses doesn't matter. } { [%csl Ge4][%cal Gf6e4,Yd2d3,Yb1c3] } 3. Nc3 { What white is hoping is that black will play the move e5 and he will be back into his familiar territory, but we aren't going to. Instead we play e6! } { [%cal Re7e5,Ge7e6] } 3... e6 { This is a very critical move as we'll see very shortly. We are going to be playing d5 shortly- this you should remember in the Bc4 line: Never play e5- play e6 and go for d5 and you will get the advantage already. } { [%csl Be6][%cal Gd7d5] } 4. d3 d5 { Here white has two general replies. } { [%csl Yd5][%cal Gc4b5,Ge4d5] } 5. exd5 (5. Bb5 Bd7 6. Nf3 a6 7. Bxc6 Bxc6 8. Ne5 { [%cal Re5c6,Gf3e5] } 8... Qd6 9. Nxc6 Qxc6 10. Qf3 { [%csl Rd5][%cal Rf3d5] } 10... Bb4 { Here white cannot capture the d5 pawn. To see what happens if he does, click the sideline on the moves list on the right -->> } { [%csl Rc3,Gd5][%cal Rb4e1,Rc6c3] } 11. Bd2 { [%csl Gc3] } (11. exd5 Bxc3+ { White cannot capture the bishop because he will lose the a1 rook. } 12. Kf1 (12. bxc3 Qxc3+ { [%cal Gc3e1,Gc3a1] }) 12... Nxd5 13. bxc3 Qxc3 { [%csl Ra1][%cal Rc3a1] } 14. Rb1 Qxc2 { [%csl Rb1][%cal Rc2b1,Gc2c1] } 15. Ra1 { You can already see that black has a HUGE advantage with the initiative, two pawns up and a strong outpost. } { [%csl Gc7,Gb7,Yd5] }) 11... Bxc3 12. Bxc3 dxe4 13. dxe4 Nxe4 { [%cal Rc3g7] } 14. Bxg7 { Although the material is even, this line gives a huge initiative to black. } 14... Rg8 15. Be5 O-O-O { So as you can see black is getting a huge initiative with two open files, a strong knight, central pawns and a safe king. } { [%csl Ge4][%cal Yg8g2,Yd8d2] } 16. O-O f5 { Freeing up the queen from defending the knight. } { [%csl Gc6] } 17. Bg3 h5 { [%cal Gh7h5] } 18. Rad1 Rxd1 { [%csl Gc2][%cal Gf3d1] } 19. Qxd1 { [%csl Gc2] } 19... h4 { [%csl Rg3] } 20. Bxh4 Nc3 { [%csl Rg2][%cal Rc6g2,Rg8g2,Bc3d1] }) 5... exd5 6. Bb3 Bb4 { Threatening ...d4. } { [%cal Rb4c3,Gd5d4] } 7. Bd2 O-O { [%cal Bf8e8] } 8. Nge2 Re8 { [%cal Ye8e1] } 9. O-O Bg4 { Time to develop our last minor piece. } 10. f3 { [%csl Rg4][%cal Rf3g4] } 10... Bc5+ { [%cal Rc5g1] } 11. Kh1 Be6 12. Nf4 { White wants to get the bishop pair. This is the critical position. When white's knight was on e2, it was guarding the d4 square. As soon as the defense has disappeared... } { [%csl Be6,Gd4][%cal Bf4e6,Ge2d4] } 12... Nd4 { Boom. } { [%csl Bd4][%cal Bc6d4] } 13. Nxe6 Rxe6 14. Re1 Rxe1+ 15. Bxe1 { And now comes the whole idea behind the e6 move: What black has been aiming for since the quick d5 is to slap that knight onto d4 and make way for the c-pawn. As soon as you do this, we play the move of the game... } { [%csl Gd4,Gc7][%cal Ye7e6,Gc6d4] } 15... c6 { C6. And what it does is it COMPLETELY shuts down that bishop and knight. This is, more or less, the idea of the e6 move. What you need to do is play e6, quick d5, and then at the right time, play your knight to d4 and completely blockade the bishop and knight with this pawn chain. It will become very hard and take a while for white to activate this bishop. You can use this time to develop your major pieces and create an attack. } { [%csl Yb7,Yc6,Yd5,Rc3,Rb3] } 16. Na4 Bd6 17. Bf2 Ne6 { We're giving white NO CHANCE to free his bishop. } 18. Qe2 b5 { [%csl Ya4][%cal Yb5a4] } 19. Nc3 a5 { [%cal Ga7a5,Ra5a4] } 20. a3 { [%cal Gb3a2] } 20... a4 21. Ba2 { [%csl Ra2] } 21... Qa5 22. Be1 Qc7 { [%csl Bh2][%cal Bc7h2] } 23. h3 { [%csl Rg3] } 23... Nh5 { [%csl Bg3][%cal Bh5g3,Bc7g3] } 24. Nd1 { [%csl Ga2][%cal Gc2c4] } 24... Nd4 { [%csl Re2,Rc2][%cal Rd4e2,Rd4c2] } 25. Qf2 { This is a horrific move. } 25... Ng3+ { [%cal Gg3h1] } 26. Kg1 Nde2+ { This is...almost a checkmate! Except white has to give up the queen! And look at that pathetic bishop on a2, who was in the prison of this amazing pawn chain. So this is the concept in the Bc4 line: Black wants to play the move e6, d5, move the knight at the right time and create the pawn chain with c6, gaining advantage. } { [%csl Ra2,Yd5,Yc6,Yb5,Ya4][%cal Ge2g1,Yg3h1,Yg3f1] } *


