1. e4 Nc6 2. Bb5 { This move has been played quite often and black should be jolly delight- the line against this is very simple and we can gain simple plan and advantage. } { [%cal Rb5c6] } 2... a6 { Here black should immediately hit this bishop with a6. Black can take or retreat. } { [%cal Ga6b5,Rb5c6,Rb5a4] } 3. Bxc6 (3. Ba4 b5 { [%cal Rb5a4] } 4. Bb3 { Can you guess the plan? ;) If you've seen the Anti-Bc4 chapter which I highly recommend you do before this one, the plan is nearly identical to the plan of that chapter. First we're going to play... } 4... Nf6 { [%csl Ge4][%cal Gf6e4,Yd2d3] } 5. d3 e6 { Remember, this is an important move in our plan to inactivate white's bishop. We're following this with d5. } { [%csl Rb3][%cal Gd7d5] } 6. Nc3 d5 7. exd5 exd5 { Once again you can see the bishop and knight are extremely dull. } { [%csl Rc3,Rb3] } 8. Bg5 { [%csl Rf6,Gd5][%cal Rg5f6,Yc3d5,Yb3d5] } 8... Bb4 { We're setting up some tactics with this move. } { [%csl Rc3][%cal Rb4c3] } 9. Bxf6 Qxf6 { [%csl Rd5] } 10. Bxd5 { White is forced to capture this pawn. To see what happens if he doesn't capture, click the sideline on the moves list over there -->> } (10. Ne2 d4 { [%csl Rc3][%cal Rb4c3,Rd4c3] } 11. a3 Ba5 { [%csl Rc3] }) 10... Bxc3+ 11. bxc3 Qxc3+ { Not only saving our knight, but white can't play Qe2 because he'll lose the a1 rook. } { [%csl Ya1,Gc6][%cal Rd1d2,Rc3a1,Gc3c6] } 12. Kf1 O-O 13. Ne2 Qc5 { [%csl Bd5][%cal Bc5d5] } 14. Bf3 Re8 { [%cal Ya8d8,Gc8d7] }) 3... bxc6 4. d4 { [%csl Ge4,Gd4] } 4... d5 { [%cal Yb1c3,Ye4e5] } 5. Nc3 (5. e5 Bf5 { We want to get our bishop outside the pawn chain. } { [%cal Ge7e6] } 6. Nf3 e6 { [%csl Yf7,Ye6,Yd5,Yc6,Gf5] } 7. O-O c5 { Following a similar plan for the Nc3 line of destroying white's center with two c-pawns. } { [%csl Rd4][%cal Rc6c5] } 8. c3 { [%cal Gd1a4] } 8... Qd7 9. Be3 cxd4 10. cxd4 Ne7 11. Nc3 Bg6 { [%cal Be7f5,Yf8e7,Ye8g8] } 12. Na4 { [%csl Gc5][%cal Ga4c5] } 12... Nf5 13. Nc5 Bxc5 14. dxc5 Nxe3 15. fxe3 { White's pawn structure is ruined. } { [%csl Re3,Re5,Rc5] } 15... Rb8 { [%csl Bb2][%cal Bb8b2] } 16. b3 Rb5 { [%csl Bc5][%cal Bb5c5] } 17. Qd4 { White's queen is acting like a sophisticated pawn. } 17... O-O 18. b4 { [%csl Gc5][%cal Gb4c5] } 18... Rfb8 { So you can see how easy it is for black to make good moves and white has trouble defending everything. } { [%csl Bb4][%cal Bb8b4] } 19. a3 a5 { [%csl Rb4,Bb1][%cal Ra5b4,Ga1b1,Bg6b1] } 20. Nh4 { [%csl Yg6][%cal Yh4g6] } 20... Be4 { [%csl Ge4][%cal Gd5e4] } 21. Nf3 { White is plan-less. } 21... axb4 22. axb4 h6 { [%csl Bg5][%cal Bh6g5,Rf3g5] } 23. Ra4 { [%csl Gb4][%cal Ga4b4,Gd4b4] } 23... Bc2 { [%cal Rc2a4] } 24. Ra2 Rxb4 { [%csl Rd4][%cal Rb4d4] } 25. Qc3 Rc4 { [%csl Bc2][%cal Bc4c2] } 26. Qa5 Be4 { Black's pieces are extremely well placed. } { [%csl Ge4,Gc4] } 27. Nd2 Rcb4 28. Nxe4 Rxe4 { [%csl Be5,Be3][%cal Be4e5,Be4e3] } 29. Ra3 { We can eat that e5 pawn anytime, so we'll activate our queen. } 29... Qe7 { [%cal Be7h4] } 30. Rfa1 Rb2 { And you can see the pathetic position of white. His 3 major pieces are on the a-file, but what on earth is he trying to do with those?? Meanwhile, black has activated both his rooks and the queen is coming on the kingside. It should be an easy win for black. } { [%csl Ra1,Ra3,Ra5][%cal Bb2g2,Bb2a2,Be4e5,Be4e3,Be7h4,Be7g5] }) 5... e6 { And now here is the plan: What black is aiming for is destroying white's center with two c-pawns. This can be prepared with Nf6-d7. } { [%csl Rc7,Rc6][%cal Rc7c5] } 6. Nf3 Nf6 7. Bg5 { [%csl Gf6][%cal Gg5d8,Re4e5] } 7... Be7 { Unpinning. } 8. e5 { This is actually what we want. } { [%cal Re5f6] } 8... Nd7 { [%csl Gc7,Gc6][%cal Rc7c5,Yd7c5] } 9. Bxe7 Qxe7 10. O-O { [%cal Gc6c5] } 10... c5 { Destroying white's center. } { [%csl Rd4] } 11. dxc5 Nxc5 12. Qe2 O-O 13. Rad1 { So it seems that white is playing just- natural moves, but white is planless, and this is the big advantage we are getting. Black's plan is very simple; what black wants to do is expand on the queenside and attack white there. } 13... Bb7 14. b4 { [%cal Gb4c5] } 14... Nd7 15. b5 { This is a big mistake. } { [%csl Be2,Bf1] } 15... axb5 16. Nxb5 c6 17. Nc3 Ba6 { [%csl Re2,Rf1][%cal Ra6f1] } *


