1. e4 Nc6 2. d4 e5 3. d5 Nce7 4. Nf3 Ng6 { Okay so far black is doing just fine } { [%cal Gf8b4,Gf8c5,Gg8f6,Yd7d6,Ye8g8] } 5. h4 { However white has this tricky move. Out of all the possibilities for white, I think this may be the most challenging for black, and especially if black doesn't know the right followup black may lose very quickly.
In the past few months I have done research on it and I think I found a good idea for black, and I don't think the 5.h4 line is as bad for black as I thought it was. } { [%csl Gh4] } 5... h5 6. Bg5 { Here you have to play Nf6. Be7 fails to d6 } { [%csl Gf6][%cal Gg8f6,Rf8e7,Rd5d6] } 6... Nf6 7. Nc3 { The logical thing to do here would probably be to put the bishop outside the pawn chain. However I think I found a good secret. } { [%cal Bf8c5] } 7... d6 { I think the secret is to keep the bishop inside on e7, preparing to swap it for white's annoying dark squared bishop.
Here I made two variations for white: Bd3 and Bb5+ } { [%csl Bf8,Rg5][%cal Bf8e7,Re7g5,Gf1d3,Gf1b5] } 8. Bb5+ (8. Bd3 Be7 9. Qd2 Nh7 { And we want to get rid of his dark squared bishop } { [%csl Rg5][%cal Yf6h7,Re7g5] } 10. Bxe7 Qxe7 11. O-O-O a6 { Good queenside counterplay for black } { [%cal Bb7b5,Bb5b4] }) 8... Bd7 9. Bxd7+ Qxd7 10. Qe2 a6 11. O-O-O { Now black can either sit tight and castle queenside, or play aggressively and get a lot of counterplay on the queenside! } { [%csl Be7][%cal Bf8e7,Rb7b5,Rb5b4,Be8c8] } *


